## 3. `player_teambet_stat` — and this one should be a **log**

The others are counters. This one the owner wants to be able to *browse*: "show
me how I bet during July". That means one row per bet, kept, not a running
total.

| Column | Must mean |
|---|---|
| `steamid64` | Who bet |
| `stamp` | When |
| `match_id` / `mapname` / round | Enough to place the bet in a session |
| `pick` | Which side they backed |
| `stake` | What they put in |
| `payout` | What came back — **0 on a loss, not NULL** |
| `outcome` | won / lost / void |

**Index on `(steamid64, stamp)` from day one.** Without it, "my bets in July" is
a full scan of every bet ever placed. This table grows faster than all the
others combined: twenty players × twenty-five rounds × ten matches a day is five
thousand rows a day, roughly two million a year. That is fine for MariaDB and
not fine without an index.

**The boring case decides whether the log adds up:** a round that is aborted, a
server that crashes mid-round, a team that forfeits. Is that a loss, a `void`
row, or no row at all? Whichever it is, it has to be consistent — otherwise the
sum in the log will not match what people remember, and the first person to add
it up will report it as a bug.

**And what is a stake?** Elo points, or a separate currency? If separate: does it
reset per season or run forever? The site needs to know before it prints a
number next to a name.

