## 5. The performance ask: defer round-end writes by 1–3 seconds

Observed on the live servers: a stutter at the exact moment the last player
dies. That is round end, which is also when the stat writes fire — the worst
possible moment, because the server is already busy with death cam, round-end
logic and the scoreboard.

Deferring the writes by a second or three would move them into the quiet part of
the round. Three things have to be true for that to work, and the middle one is
where this usually goes wrong:

**Capture at round end. Write later.** The numbers are correct at the moment the
round ends and stop being correct immediately afterwards — freeze time, warmup,
and score resets all follow. If the delay is implemented by reading the data
later, the data will be wrong. Snapshot the values into a queue at round end;
delay only the flush.

**Players disconnect at round end.** More than at any other moment. If the write
path reads from live player objects two seconds later, some of them are gone and
their round is lost silently. This is the same point as above, and it is the one
that bites: the fix is not "delay the write", it is "separate capture from
write".

**Flush on the way out.** The queue must be emptied on round start, map change,
and shutdown. A deferred write that a map change eats is worse than a stutter —
one is a hitch nobody reports, the other is a round that never happened.

If the writes are currently many separate statements, **batching them into one
transaction is likely the bigger win than the delay itself.** Both together
would be ideal.

